WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:09.750
I have been yearning for a light transport
paper, and goodness, was I ecstatic when reading

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.940
this one.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.190
And by the end of the video, I hope you will
be too.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.740
And now, we only have to go through just about
30 years of light transport research.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.560
As some of you know, if we immerse ourselves
into the art of light transport simulations,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.750
we can use our computer to simulate millions
and millions of light rays, and calculate

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.590
how they get absorbed or scattered off of
our objects in a virtual scene.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.700
Initially, we start out with a really noisy
image, and as we add more rays, the image

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.400
gets clearer and clearer over time.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.300
The time it takes for these images to clean
up depends on the complexity of the geometry

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.780
and our material models, but it typically
takes a while.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.530
This microplanet scene mostly contains vegetation,
which are matte objects, these we also refer

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.400
to as diffuse materials, these typically converge
very quickly.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.490
As you see, we get meaningful progress on
the entirety of the image within the first

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.820
two minutes of the rendering process.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.240
And remember, in light transport simulations,
noise is public enemy number one.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.420
This used a technique called path tracing.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.840
Let’s refer to it as the okay technique.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.870
And now, let’s try to use path tracing,
this okay technique to render this torus in

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.700
a glass enclosure.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.070
This is the first two minutes of the rendering
process, and it does not look anything like

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.370
the previous scene.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.750
The previous one was looking pretty smooth
after two minutes, whereas here, you see,

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.280
this is indeed looking grim.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:45.090
We have lots of these fireflies, which will
take us up to a few days of computation time

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.820
to clean up, even if we have a modern, powerful
machine.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.270
So, why did this happen?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.880
The reason for this is that there are tricky
cases for specular light transport that take

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.520
many-many millions, if not billions of light
rays to compute properly.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.450
Specular here means mirror-like materials,
those can get tricky, and this torus that

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.619
has been enclosed in there is also not doing
too well.

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.840
So this was path tracing, the okay technique.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.840
Now let’s try a better technique called
Metropolis Light Transport!

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.900
This method is the result of a decade of research
and is much better in dealing with difficult

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.900
scenes.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.190
This particular variant is a proud Hungarian
algorithm by a scientist called Csaba Kelemen

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.780
and his colleagues at the Technical University
of Budapest.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.920
For instance, here is a snippet of our earlier
paper on a similarly challenging scene.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.240
This is how the okay path tracer did, and
in the same amount of time, this is what Metropolis

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.540
Light Transport, the better technique could
do!

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:55.150
This was a lot more efficient, so let’s
see how it does with the torus.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.260
Now that’s unexpected!

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.730
This is indeed a notoriously difficult scene
to render, even for Metropolis Light Transport,

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.319
the better technique.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:09.180
As you see, the reflected light patterns that
we also refer to as caustics on the floor

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.630
are much cleaner, but the torus is still not
giving up.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:19.010
Let’s jump another 15 years of light transport
research, and use a technique that goes by

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.120
the name Manifold Exploration.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.970
Let’s call this the best technique.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.980
Wow.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:33.250
Look at how beautifully it improves the image,
it is not only much cleaner, but also converges

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.600
much more gracefully.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:41.200
It doesn’t go from a noisy image to a slightly
less noisy image, but almost immediately gives

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.940
us a solid baseline, and new, cleaned up light
paths appear over time.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.510
This technique is from 2012, and it truly
is mind boggling how good it is.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.580
This technique is so difficult to understand
and implement, that to the best of my knowledge,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:03.739
the number of people who can and have implemented
it properly is exactly one.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.700
And that one person is Wenzel Jakob, one of
the best minds in the game, and believe it

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.660
or not, he wrote this method as a PhD student
in light transport research.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:19.359
And today, as a professor at EPFL Switzerland,
he and his colleagues set out to create a

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:26.409
technique that is as good as manifold exploration,
the best technique, but is much simpler.

00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:30.240
Well, good luck with that, I thought when
skimming through the paper.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.860
Let’s see how it did.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.309
For instance, we have some caustics at the
bottom of a pool of water, as expected, lots

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:43.319
of firefly noise with the okay path tracer,
and now, hold on to your papers, and here

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:45.430
is the new technique.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:46.870
Just look at that!

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.550
It can do so much better in the same amount
of time!

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:56.009
Let’s also have a look at this scene with
lots and lots of specular microgeometry, or

00:04:56.009 --> 00:04:58.270
in other words, glints.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.680
This is also a nightmare to render.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.309
With the okay path tracer, we have lots of
flickering from one frame to the next, and

00:05:05.309 --> 00:05:08.479
here you see the result with the new technique.

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:09.839
Perfect.

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:15.320
So it is indeed possible to take the best
technique, manifold exploration, and reimagine

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.270
it in a way that ordinary humans can also
implement.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:24.020
Huge congratulations to the authors on this
work, that I think, is a crown achievement

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.360
in light transport research.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.159
And that’s why I was ecstatic when I first
read through this incredible paper.

00:05:31.159 --> 00:05:35.360
Make sure to have a look at the paper and
you will see how they borrowed a nice little

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.710
trick from a recent work in nuclear physics
to tackle this problem.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.589
The presentation of the paper and the talk
video with the details is also brilliant and

00:05:44.589 --> 00:05:47.569
I urge you to have a look at it in the video
description.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:53.360
This whole thing got me so excited I was barely
able to fall asleep for several days now.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:54.710
What a time to be alive!

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:59.610
Now, while we look through some more results
from this paper, if you feel a little stranded

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.779
at home and are thinking that this light transport
thing is pretty cool, and you would like to

00:06:03.779 --> 00:06:08.149
learn more about it, I held a Master-level
course on this topic at the Technical University

00:06:08.149 --> 00:06:09.169
of Vienna.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:13.409
Since I was always teaching it to a handful
of motivated students, I thought that the

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:17.990
teachings shouldn’t only be available for
the privileged few who can afford a college

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.740
education, but the teachings should be available
for everyone.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.729
Free education for everyone, that’s what
I want.

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:30.590
So, the course is available free of charge
for everyone, no strings attached, so make

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.430
sure to click the link in the video description
to get started.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:37.850
We write a full light simulation program from
scratch there, and learn about physics, the

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.039
world around us, and more.

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:46.050
Also, note that my former PhD advisor, Michael
Wimmer is looking to hire a postdoctoral researcher

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:50.809
in this area, which is an amazing opportunity
to push this field forward.

00:06:50.809 --> 00:07:43.179
The link is available in the video description.

00:07:43.179 --> 00:07:45.802
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

